//! KODAK AEROCOLOR IV Negative Film 2460, KODAK Publication AS-2652 (October 2009): its curves,
//! read from the vector graphics of the datasheet's PDF.
//!
//! Generated by `tools/film-curves/extract_curves.py` from `kodak_as2652_aerocolor_iv_2460.json`; do
//! not edit by hand. Source PDF:
//! <https://web.archive.org/web/20260107194118/https://www.kodak.com/content/pdfs/KODAK-AEROCOLOR-IV-Negative-Film-2460-datasheet-en.pdf>
//! (SHA-256 `de0bbc31dcde97f53f8a736e1e5e7becf8ead15911613936410ed2d3a5287adf`).
//!
//! Kodak's own URL for this sheet now gives 404; the URL is the Internet Archive's copy.
//!
//! An aerial film with no integral color masking (the sheet's words). It prints three
//! characteristic curves for three development times; these are "Developed for Low
//! Contrast": Process C-41 with its standard 3:15 development (or AN-6, 137 s, 38.9 °C),
//! daylight exposure, Status M densitometry. The sheet gives this development a gamma of
//! 0.63 and an aerial film speed (EAFS) of 80.
//!
//! The sheet gives no Log H ref: aerial film speeds aren't pictorial ones. -0.84 is assumed,
//! where Kodak's ISO 100 still films put a grey card (Gold 100, Ektar 100): log10(0.8 / 100)
//! + 1.26. Rebranded as a pictorial film, it is sold rated ISO 100.
//!
//! Spectral sensitivity (page 5): effective exposure 1/25 s, Status M, density 0.3 above
//! D-min. Spectral dye density (page 6): typical densities of a midscale neutral subject and
//! D-min.
//!
//! Page coordinates: the plots' axes are calibrated from their tick marks.

